<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/earth-interior">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    No drill has passed ~12 km — a pinprick through a 6,371 km radius. Everything deeper is
    known the way doctors know hearts: by listening. <strong>Seismic waves</strong> from
    earthquakes ring through the planet; how they speed up, slow down, bend or vanish maps the
    interior in astonishing detail.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the geotherm — temperature from crust to core; note the dip around 400–660 km" widget="plotter" config={geothermCfg} />

  <h2>The three layers that matter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Crust — 5 to 70 km.</strong> Ocean floors are thin basalt (~5–10 km); continents ride on thicker, lighter granite (30–70 km). We have never left this layer by drilling.</li>
    <li><strong>Mantle — to 2,890 km.</strong> Solid rock that flows, very slowly — a few cm a year — driving plate tectonics. Olivine-rich; the 400 and 660 km "dips" in the curve are mineral phase changes, not pauses in heating.</li>
    <li><strong>Core — 2,890 to 6,371 km.</strong> Iron and nickel. The outer core is liquid (S-waves die there — the shadow zone proved it), and its churning generates Earth's magnetic field. The inner core is solid: hotter still, but crushed by pressure past iron's melting point.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the numbers were won</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>S-waves are the proof.</strong> Shear waves cannot cross liquid. Their "shadow" on the far side of the planet from big quakes revealed the liquid outer core.</li>
    <li><strong>Reflections time the layers.</strong> Waves bounce off boundaries like echo-sounding; arrival times at thousands of stations worldwide fix depths to within kilometres.</li>
    <li><strong>Meteorites are samples.</strong> Iron meteorites are thought to resemble the core's composition — our only direct "core specimen".</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Perspective check: the centre sits near 5,400 °C — about the temperature of the Sun's visible
    surface. The coolest place on the journey is where you are.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, An Introduction to Geology; USGS earthquake science; standard geotherm models.</p>
</TopicPage>
